WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.434 line:0%
-It's in the cloud.

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.437 line:0%
It's in the cloud. OJ!

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:07.139 line:0%
It's in the cloud!

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:08.942 line:0%
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:15.848 line:0%
[ Rumbling ]

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.617 line:0%
-Yeah, no, no, no.

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:20.286 line:0%
Run, OJ, run!

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:22.155 line:0%
[ Dramatic music plays ]

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.462 line:0%
[ Growling ]

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.669
-Welcome back to "Late Night,"
everybody!

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.271
We're here with Jordan Peele.

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.706
That was a clip from your
excellent film "Nope."

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:43.343
I saw you last night.
We mentioned your cast.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.744
I've talked to you before,
as a director, as a writer,

00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:47.980
you put together so many
elements to make a film.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.682
One of the things that seems
coolest to me is

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.083
when you put together
your casts.

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.953
And last night, you presented
an award for Keke Palmer.

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:57.757
She won the New York Film
Critics Circle Award

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.158
for best actor -- best actress.
-Very prestigious,

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:01.160
very prestigious,
very happy for her.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.129
-And it was lovely. You said
some lovely words about her

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.730
and how much
she's given to film.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.699
-She is truly incredible.

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:08.835
It was one of these
performances,

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.636
and everybody's amazing in the
film, but one of these things

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:13.673
where you could just feel
the whole crew and cast

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:15.407
could feel something special
was happening.

00:01:15.407 --> 00:01:17.911
And she was creating a character
in Emerald Haywood

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.345
that we hadn't seen before.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:21.480
So I was just so happy
to present that to her.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.482
She's a dear friend.

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:26.719
-You've curated a series of
films for --

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:27.887
Let me get the name right.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.123
"The Lost Rider."
There's more to it, right?

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.525
-Yes. I believe it's --

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.460
-"A Chronicle of
Hollywood Sacrifice."

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.530
-Thank you, thank you.
I wrote that, I wrote that.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.699
-So, this is --
You're showing "Nope,"

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.001
but you also picked
a bunch of other films.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.504
Are they tied to the inspiration
for "Nope"?

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:47.039
-Yeah, you know, a lot of
people, you know, ask what

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:49.676
"Nope" is about, and trying to
understand the meaning.

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:51.911
It's one of these things where
it's like I feel like if I could

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.847
really whittle it down
to two sentences,

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:56.749
I wouldn't have made the movie.
-Yeah.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:02:00.119
-But what I could do is sort of
put a bunch of films together

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:02.655
that I think give good context
to what the movie's about.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.891
-And that must be exciting.
And it also must be a little --

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:07.360
especially when -- you know,
it's one thing to tell friends,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.428
"Oh, it was a little bit
of this."

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:09.829
But now you actually put
together a list

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:11.231
and people are gonna go
and be able to see these films.

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:12.298
-It's a crazy list.
-Some of them I know.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.966
"The Wiz," "The Wizard of Oz."

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:16.135
Those make a lot of sense
for me.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:17.970
"Friday the 13th:
The Final Chapter."

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.105
-Yes.
-I can't keep them straight.

00:02:20.105 --> 00:02:22.075
Was that one particularly
different for you?

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.910
-Yes, well, first of all,
you'll see

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.512
there's a few Corey Feldman
movies.

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:27.013
-Yes.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.648
We talked about Corey Feldman
the last time you were here.

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:30.282
-So I have a new friendship
with Corey Feldman.

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:31.417
-Since the last time
you were here?

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.718
-Very proud of this.
-It's very exciting.

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:36.922
-Yeah, so he invited me and
my producer to his house.

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:39.258
We went to his house,
which was awesome.

00:02:39.258 --> 00:02:42.428
He had memorabilia and stuff.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.331
Sat us down, and he's, you know,
just this cool, you know --

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.534
By the way, if you don't know
Corey Feldman, means you --

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.870
I mean, first of all,
[bleep] off, you're a kid.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.271
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:55.741
But this is like one of the
greatest teen icons of all time.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:58.411
-Yeah, that is -- the
heartbreaking thing is realizing

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.147
kids don't know someone
you knew as a kid.

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.883
Not someone you knew
as an adult.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:07.787
-But anyway, so, you know, we
visit, and he has this film

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.989
that he showed called
"The Birthday."

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.926
-Yeah.
-That was unreleased,

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:17.863
and it is -- If you are like
a cinephile who is just into,

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:22.234
like, a complete "what the F did
I just watch" experience,

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:24.203
it is like one of --
it is this treasure.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:27.273
And so the thing is kind of
leading up to this missing film,

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:30.442
because the movie "Nope" in
itself is

00:03:30.442 --> 00:03:33.045
about exploitation and erasure.

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:38.751
And anyway, so I'm very geeked
to have this relationship

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.487
with Feldman, and he's going to
be doing the Q&amp;A as well.

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:44.624
-And how was -- He must have
been excited when you told him,

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.626
"Hey, this movie you just showed
me at your house,

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:47.793
I want to bring it out,
I want to show it

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:49.495
at this retrospective."

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.563
-I think he's thrilled.
-That's great.

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:54.600
-I think he's thrilled.
And no, he's --

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.636
I'm happy to consider him
a friend.

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.938
There was a -- I saw, you know,
there was a whole thing where he

00:03:59.938 --> 00:04:05.143
was auctioning this -- the ear
that he wore as Teddy

00:04:05.143 --> 00:04:09.916
on "Stand by Me," and I guess I
very creepily bought it.

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.851
I guess that's --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.219
-You guess? It feels like you
definitely own that now.

00:04:14.219 --> 00:04:17.190
-Yeah, but the creep--
I'm saying it out loud.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.491
I realize how creepy it was.

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.658
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:21.327
That's not the thing
you should share.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:23.129
On "Seth Meyers."
-Yeah, you should say,

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.797
"I bought a piece of
memorabilia"

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.265
and then just leave it at that.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.234
-But at the end of this thing,
I mentioned it to him.

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:30.336
And he was like,
"Wait, you got the ear?"

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:32.004
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.906
And he was like --
[ Laughs ]

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:36.809
He was like,
"Man, I'm so glad it was you."

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:39.645
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.748
It's great. So we have a bond.
-That's very sweet.

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:43.048
-Very cool.

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:45.251
-That's so cool that you're
doing that retrospective.

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:47.720
Again, I feel like anybody
who hasn't seen

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.955
"Nope" should see it.
I've seen it twice.

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:51.357
I feel like there's
so many things

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.926
to find with repeat viewings.

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.094
It's a wonderful film.
-Thank you.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:56.295
-And it's always so great
to have you here, buddy.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:58.097
Congratulations.
Keep coming back.

00:04:58.097 --> 00:04:59.131
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:00.165
-Jordan Peele, everybody.

00:05:00.165 --> 00:05:02.034
"Nope" is streaming now on
Peacock.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.203
Go watch it. We'll be right back
with Melissa Rauch.

